210
00:15:44,999 --> 00:15:49,659
kinds of ref: what the deletion removed,
what it left behind in some clone, and

211
00:15:49,699 --> 00:15:54,180
which ordinary command turned what was
left into a new branch on the server.

212
00:15:54,220 --> 00:15:59,117
It says how you would find out which clone
did it. Then it separates the changes by

213
00:15:59,157 --> 00:16:03,936
layer: what each engineer does in their
clone, what can be configured in Git, and

214
00:16:03,976 --> 00:16:08,397
what a server-side rule could refuse.
It also names the cost of the obvious

215
00:16:08,437 --> 00:16:09,468
configuration fix.

216
00:16:11,308 --> 00:16:14,235
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

217
00:16:14,575 --> 00:16:19,584
Each remote has its own URL, refspec
and namespace of remote-tracking refs.

218
00:16:19,624 --> 00:16:23,825
In a triangular setup I fetch
from upstream and push to origin.

219
00:16:23,865 --> 00:16:29,480
branch.<name>.pushRemote, remote.pushDefault,
branch.<name>.remote and origin decide

220
00:16:29,520 --> 00:16:31,567
the push remote, in that order.

221
00:16:31,907 --> 00:16:35,539
A fetch never deletes a
remote-tracking ref unless it prunes.

222
00:16:35,579 --> 00:16:40,174
Pruning deletes stale remote-tracking
refs and their reflogs and leaves local

223
00:16:40,214 --> 00:16:41,860
branches alone, marked gone.

224
00:16:42,200 --> 00:16:45,342
A push from such a branch
re-creates it on the server.

225
00:16:45,382 --> 00:16:48,936
Pruning is also forgetting, so
automatic pruning has a price.

226
00:16:50,776 --> 00:16:55,162
Read sections 12.10 and
12.11 of Chapter 12.

227
00:16:55,502 --> 00:17:01,812
